<section class="article-header"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/">International Clients</a> / Remote Property Purchase</div><div class="tag">Real Estate Law · Power of Attorney</div><h1>Buying Property in Turkey Remotely: Can the Process Be Completed by Power of Attorney?</h1><p class="article-summary">A foreign buyer does not always need to be physically present for every step of a Turkish property transaction. Representation can simplify the process, but the power of attorney must match the exact transaction and the buyer should retain control over the legal and financial steps.</p><div class="article-meta">Published: 8 September 2026 · Ozan Ferhat Şen, Attorney at Law</div></div></section>
<section class="wrap article-layout"><article class="article-body">
<p>Remote property purchases are increasingly common where the buyer lives abroad or cannot remain in Turkey for the entire transaction. A properly prepared power of attorney may allow a representative to carry out important steps, but it should not be treated as a generic document.</p>
<h2>1. Decide what the representative actually needs to do</h2>
<p>A property transaction may involve title-deed procedures, signing documents, obtaining records, dealing with banks or tax-related formalities and receiving official notifications. The power of attorney should be tailored to the steps the representative is expected to perform.</p>
<h2>2. Avoid broader powers than the transaction requires</h2>
<p>Authority to purchase a specified property is different from unrestricted authority to sell property, create a mortgage, borrow money, receive sale proceeds or dispose of other assets. The document should not become wider merely because a template is convenient.</p>
<h2>3. If the power of attorney is issued abroad, prepare the wording first</h2>
<p>The buyer may use a Turkish consulate or, depending on the country and applicable authentication rules, a foreign notary route. The key practical point is that the wording should be checked for Turkish use before the appointment. A formally valid foreign document can still be inadequate if the necessary transaction authority is missing.</p>
<h2>4. Different countries, same practical question</h2>
<p>A buyer sitting in Germany may search <em>Vollmacht Türkei Immobilie</em>; a Russian-speaking buyer may search <span lang="ru">доверенность на покупку недвижимости в Турции</span>; an Arabic-speaking buyer may search <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">توكيل لشراء عقار في تركيا</span>. The search language may differ, but the practical question is the same: can someone in Turkey complete the transaction lawfully on my behalf, and what must the power of attorney actually say?</p>
<p>The answer depends on the intended Turkish transaction, the country where the document is issued and the formal route used to make it effective in Turkey. The safest approach is to prepare the required Turkish transaction powers before the foreign notary or consular appointment, not after.</p>
<h2>5. The representative should not replace independent due diligence</h2>
<p>Representation answers the question of who can act. Due diligence answers whether the buyer should proceed. Title ownership, encumbrances, zoning, permits and contractual risk should still be reviewed separately.</p>
<h2>6. Payment control remains important</h2>
<p>Where purchase funds are transferred from abroad, the buyer should understand who is authorised to receive money, when payment becomes due and how payment is coordinated with the title transfer. The legal structure should avoid unnecessary uncertainty between payment and registration.</p>
<h2>7. The title deed appointment is only one part of the transaction</h2>
<p>Before the final transfer, documents may need to be gathered, translated or authenticated. The property may require title and permit review. The sale agreement may need revision. A remote transaction works best when these steps are organised as one process rather than as isolated appointments.</p>
<h2>8. What can often be coordinated in Turkey?</h2>
<ul><li>Review of title records and transaction documents</li><li>Preparation or review of transaction-specific power-of-attorney wording</li><li>Coordination of sworn translation and notarial formalities where required</li><li>Preparation for land-registry procedures</li><li>Communication with the seller, developer or relevant institution</li><li>Contract review and transaction sequencing</li></ul>
<h2>9. What may still require personal involvement?</h2>
<p>The answer depends on the transaction, institution and document. Banks, consulates or other authorities may require personal identification or attendance for particular steps. A remote-purchase plan should therefore distinguish clearly between what can be delegated and what cannot.</p>
<h2>A useful rule: prepare the representation structure before money moves</h2>
<p>The buyer gains little from discovering after payment that the representative lacks authority to complete a key step. The power of attorney, due diligence and payment sequence should be designed together before the transaction becomes difficult to reverse.</p>
